Our
work
uniquely
addresses
all
five
gaps
through
a
comprehensive
framework
combining
priority
awareness,
adaptive
control,
multi-AP
analysis,
and
theoretical
validation.

\hypertarget{our-contributions}{%
\subsection{Our
Contributions}\label{our-contributions}}

This
paper
makes
the
following
contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item
  We
  propose
  \textbf{AS-CAC},
  an
  airtime-based
  soft
  admission
  control
  framework
  with
  priority-aware
  thresholds
  that
  maximizes
  channel
  utilization
  while
  maintaining
  strict
  QoS
  guarantees.
\item
  We
  introduce
  \textbf{AS-CAC+},
  an
  adaptive
  enhancement
  that
  dynamically
  adjusts
  admission
  thresholds
  based
  on
  real-time
  network
  health
  indicators,
  achieving
  near-optimal
  efficiency.
\item
  We
  develop
  a
  \textbf{multi-rate
  Erlang
  loss
  analytical
  model}
  to
  validate
  simulation
  results
  and
  provide
  theoretical
  foundation.
\item
  We
  conduct
  comprehensive
  \textbf{ns-3
  simulations}
  across
  multiple
  scenarios
  including
  single-AP,
  multi-AP
  with
  CCI,
  and
  multi-AP
  with
  ACI,
  demonstrating
  19.2\%
  throughput
  improvement
  and
  97.4\%
  utilization.
\item
  We
  provide
  \textbf{open-source
  implementation}
  and
  detailed
  performance
  analysis
  suitable
  for
  deployment
  in
  enterprise
  WLANs.
\end{enumerate}

The
remainder
of
this
paper
is
organized
as
follows:
Section
II
reviews
related
literature.
Section
III
presents
the
system
model
and
problem
formulation.
Section
IV
describes
the
proposed
AS-CAC
and
AS-CAC+
mechanisms.
Section
V
presents
the
analytical
model.
Section
VI
details
the
simulation
setup
and
results.
Section
VII
discusses
findings
and
implications.
Section
VIII
concludes
the
paper.

\hypertarget{related-work}{%
\section{Related
Work}\label{related-work}}

\hypertarget{ieee-802.11ax-enhancements}{%
\subsection{IEEE
802.11ax
Enhancements}\label{ieee-802.11ax-enhancements}}

The
IEEE
802.11ax
standard
introduces
several
key
innovations
to
address
dense
deployment
challenges.
Bellalta
\cite{2}
provides
a
comprehensive
overview
of
Wi-Fi
6
features
including
OFDMA,
which
enables
simultaneous
transmission
to
multiple
users
by
dividing
the
channel
into
Resource
Units
(RUs).
Mozaffariahrar
et
al.
\cite{13}
survey
Wi-Fi
6
technologies,
highlighting
improvements
in
spectral
efficiency
and
latency
reduction.
However,
these
PHY/MAC
enhancements
alone
cannot
prevent
saturation
when
offered
load
exceeds
capacity.

Agbeve
et
al.
\cite{7}
evaluate
uplink
OFDMA
performance,
demonstrating
throughput
gains
in
multi-user
scenarios.
Liu
et
al.
\cite{11}
optimize
random
access
mechanisms
for
UL
OFDMA.
While
these
works
improve
channel
access
efficiency,
they
do
not
address
admission
control---the
fundamental
question
of
which
flows
should
be
admitted.

\hypertarget{spatial-reuse-and-interference-management}{%
\subsection{Spatial
Reuse
and
Interference
Management}\label{spatial-reuse-and-interference-management}}

Spatial
reuse
mechanisms
in
Wi-Fi
6
aim
to
increase
network
capacity
by
allowing
concurrent
transmissions.
Wilhelmi
et
al.
\cite{17}
analyze
spatial
reuse
performance,
showing
significant
gains
in
dense
deployments.
Bardou
et
al.
\cite{1}
propose
a
multi-armed
bandit
approach
for
dynamic
spatial
reuse
optimization.
Lanante
and
Roy
\cite{10}
provide
theoretical
analysis
of
OBSS\_PD-based
spatial
reuse,
identifying
optimal
threshold
settings.

Knitter
and
Kays
\cite{9}
extend
spatial
reuse
analysis
to
Wi-Fi
7,
providing
insights
applicable
to
future
standards.
Dogan-Tusha
et
al.
\cite{4}
conduct
extensive
measurements
of
interference
in
6
GHz
Wi-Fi
6E
networks,
quantifying
CCI
and
ACI
impacts.
MediaTek
\cite{12}
discusses
anti-interference
technologies
for
Wi-Fi
6/6E.

While
spatial
reuse
increases
capacity,
it
operates
orthogonally
to
admission
control.
Our
AS-CAC
framework
complements
spatial
reuse
by
controlling
flow
admission,
while
spatial
reuse
optimizes
coexistence
of
admitted
flows.

\hypertarget{admission-control-in-wlans}{%
\subsection{Admission
Control
in
WLANs}\label{admission-control-in-wlans}}

Classical
admission
control
for
WLANs
dates
to
early
802.11
standards.
Bianchi
\cite{3}
provides
foundational
analysis
of
the
DCF,
establishing
performance
models
still
relevant
today.
Kaufman
\cite{8}
introduces
the
Erlang
loss
model
for
shared
resources,
which
we
extend
to
multi-rate
airtime-based
admission.

Traditional
CAC
schemes
use
association
counts
or
bandwidth
estimates.
However,
these
metrics
fail
to
capture
actual
resource
consumption
in
modern
WLANs
where
traffic
heterogeneity
is
extreme.
A
VoIP
flow
consuming
64
kbps
requires
vastly
different
airtime
than
a
4K
video
stream
at
the
same
PHY
rate
due
to
packet
size
and
overhead
differences.

Bhat
et
al.
\cite{16}
propose
feedback-based
congestion
control
for
airtime
fairness
but
focus
on
rate
adaptation
rather
than
admission
decisions.
Rehman
et
al.
\cite{15}
enhance
spectrum
access
with
heuristic
back-off
mechanisms
for
OFDMA,
operating
at
frame
timescales
rather
than
flow
timescales.

\textbf{Gap}:
Existing
schemes
use
static
thresholds
that
either
waste
capacity
(conservative)
or
fail
QoS
(aggressive).
Our
AS-CAC+
addresses
this
through
dynamic
adaptation.

\hypertarget{multi-ap-coordination}{%
\subsection{Multi-AP
Coordination}\label{multi-ap-coordination}}

Dense
deployments
increasingly
feature
overlapping
BSSs,
necessitating
multi-AP
coordination.
Zhang
et
al.
\cite{18}
apply
deep
reinforcement
learning
(DDPG)
for
multi-AP
access
control,
achieving
good
performance
but
requiring
extensive
training
data
and
computational
resources.
ZTE
\cite{19}
discusses
multi-AP
home
network
technologies,
focusing
on
seamless
handover
rather
than
admission
control.

Garcia-Rodriguez
et
al.
\cite{5}
preview
Wi-Fi
7
(802.11be)
multi-link
operation,
enabling
simultaneous
transmission
across
multiple
bands.
While
promising,
multi-link
operation
does
not
eliminate
the
need
for
admission
control---it
shifts
the
problem
to
coordinating
admission
across
links.

\textbf{Gap}:
Limited
research
addresses
admission
control
in
multi-AP
scenarios
with
explicit
CCI/ACI
modeling.
Our
work
evaluates
AS-CAC
in
both
CCI
and
ACI
scenarios.

\hypertarget{summary-and-positioning}{%
\subsection{Summary
and
Positioning}\label{summary-and-positioning}}

Table
\protect\hyperlink{tab:gaps}{1}
(Section
I)
summarizes
five
key
gaps
in
existing
literature:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Static
  thresholds}
  waste
  capacity
  or
  fail
  QoS
\item
  \textbf{No
  priority
  awareness}
  for
  QoS
  classes
\item
  \textbf{Single-AP
  focus},
  limited
  multi-AP
  analysis
\item
  \textbf{Lack
  of
  analytical
  validation}
\item
  \textbf{Ignoring
  airtime}
  as
  the
  fundamental
  resource
\end{itemize}

Our
AS-CAC
framework
uniquely
addresses
all
five
gaps
through:
(1)
priority-based
soft
thresholds,
(2)
adaptive
dynamic
adjustment
(AS-CAC+),
(3)
multi-AP
CCI/ACI
evaluation,
(4)
Erlang
loss
analytical
model,
and
(5)
direct
airtime
measurement.
This
comprehensive
approach
distinguishes
our
work
from
prior
art.

\hypertarget{system-model}{%
\section{System
Model}\label{system-model}}

\hypertarget{network-architecture}{%
\subsection{Network
Architecture}\label{network-architecture}}

We
consider
a
dense
802.11ax
network
deployment
with
multiple
overlapping
Basic
Service
Sets
(BSSs).
Each
BSS
consists
of
one
Access
Point
(AP)
and
multiple
associated
stations
(STAs).
The
network
operates
in
the
5
GHz
band
with
80
MHz
channel
bandwidth,
supporting
Wi-Fi
6
features
including
OFDMA
and
MU-MIMO
\cite{6}.

\begin{figure}
\hypertarget{fig:network_topology}{%
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth,height=\textheight]{../results/graphs/single_ap_topology.png}
\caption{NS-3
network
topology
visualization
for
a
dense
WLAN
with
heterogeneous
traffic
types
(VoIP,
Video,
Bursty).}\label{fig:network_topology}
}
\end{figure}

\hypertarget{traffic-model}{%
\subsection{Traffic
Model}\label{traffic-model}}

Traffic
flows
belong
to
three
primary
classes,
each
mapped
to
appropriate
Access
Categories
(ACs):

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{VoIP
  (AC\_VO)}:
  G.711
  codec,
  64
  kbps
  constant
  bitrate,
  160-byte
  packets
  every
  20
  ms,
  strict
  delay
  requirement
  \(< 30\)
  ms.
\item
  \textbf{Video
  (AC\_VI)}:
  Variable
  bitrate
  (VBR)
  streaming,
  3
  Mbps
  average,
  1200-byte
  packets,
  delay
  tolerance
  \(< 100\)
  ms.
\item
  \textbf{Best-Effort
  (AC\_BE)}:
  Bursty
  On-Off
  traffic,
  5
  Mbps
  peak
  rate
  during
  ON
  periods,
  delay-tolerant.
\end{itemize}

\hypertarget{airtime-calculation}{%
\subsection{Airtime
Calculation}\label{airtime-calculation}}

For
a
flow
of
class
\(c\)
with
application
rate
\(R_c\)
and
average
PHY
rate
\(R_{phy}^c\),
the
airtime
fraction
consumed
is:

\[\alpha_c = \frac{R_c}{\eta \cdot R_{phy}^c}
\label{eq:airtime}\]

where
\(\eta \in (0,1]\)
accounts
for
MAC/PHY
overhead
including
SIFS,
DIFS,
backoff,
and
acknowledgments.
For
Wi-Fi
6
with
OFDMA,
\(\eta \approx 0.6\)-\(0.7\)
in
dense
scenarios
\cite{2}.

With
\(n_c\)
active
flows
of
class
\(c\),
total
airtime
utilization
is:

\[A = \sum_{c \in \{VO, VI, BE\}} n_c \cdot \alpha_c
\label{eq:total_airtime}\]

\hypertarget{problem-formulation}{%
\subsection{Problem
Formulation}\label{problem-formulation}}

The
admission
control
problem
is
to
maximize
network
utility
while
maintaining
QoS:

\[\begin{aligned}
\max_{n_{VO}, n_{VI}, n_{BE}} \quad & \sum_{c} w_c \cdot n_c \cdot R_c \label{eq:objective}\\
\text{subject to} \quad & A \leq \theta_{max} \label{eq:constraint1}\\
& D_{VO} \leq 30 \text{ ms} \label{eq:constraint2}\\
& D_{VI} \leq 100 \text{ ms} \label{eq:constraint3}
\end{aligned}\]

where
\(w_c\)
are
priority
weights,
\(\theta_{max}\)
is
the
maximum
safe
utilization
threshold,
and
\(D_c\)
represents
average
delay
for
class
\(c\).

\hypertarget{proposed-as-cac-framework}{%
\section{Proposed
AS-CAC
Framework}\label{proposed-as-cac-framework}}

\hypertarget{hard-cac-baseline}{%
\subsection{Hard
CAC
(Baseline)}\label{hard-cac-baseline}}

Traditional
hard
CAC
applies
a
single
threshold
\(\theta\)
(typically
0.8)
to
all
traffic:

\[\text{Admit flow } f \text{ if } A + \alpha_f \leq \theta
\label{eq:hard_cac}\]

While
simple,
this
approach
is
overly
conservative,
leaving
20\%
of
channel
capacity
unused
to
maintain
a
safety
margin
\cite{8}.

\hypertarget{soft-cac-priority-based}{%
\subsection{Soft
CAC
(Priority-Based)}\label{soft-cac-priority-based}}

Our
Soft
CAC
mechanism
introduces
class-specific
thresholds:

\[\theta_c = \begin{cases}
0.90 & \text{if } c = VO \\
0.80 & \text{if } c = VI \\
0.95 & \text{if } c = BE
\end{cases}
\label{eq:soft_thresholds}\]

A
flow
of
class
\(c\)
is
admitted
if:

\[A + \alpha_c \leq \theta_c
\label{eq:soft_admission}\]

This
allows
best-effort
traffic
to
``fill
the
gaps''
up
to
95\%
utilization
without
compromising
VoIP
(protected
at
90\%)
or
Video
(protected
at
80\%).

\hypertarget{as-cac-adaptive}{%
\subsection{AS-CAC+
(Adaptive)}\label{as-cac-adaptive}}

AS-CAC+
enhances
Soft
CAC
with
dynamic
threshold
adjustment
based
on
network
health.
We
simulate
Packet
Error
Rate
(PER)
as
a
function
of
utilization:

\[PER(A) = \begin{cases}
0.001 & \text{if } A \leq 0.80 \\
0.01 & \text{if } 0.80 < A \leq 0.90 \\
0.05 & \text{if } 0.90 < A \leq 0.95 \\
0.15 & \text{if } A > 0.95
\end{cases}
\label{eq:per}\]

\emph{Rationale
for
PER}:
We
select
Packet
Error
Rate
(PER)
as
the
primary
network
health
metric
rather
than
Channel
Clean
Assessment
(CCA)
busy
ratio
or
Signal-to-Noise
Ratio
(SNR).
In
dense
OFDMA
environments,
CCA
often
underestimates
congestion
due
to
efficient
spatial
reuse
colors
and
OBSS\_PD
mechanisms
\cite{10}.
Conversely,
SNR
captures
link
quality
but
fails
to
account
for
collision-induced
losses
in
saturated
contention
periods.
PER
provides
a
direct,
end-to-end
measure
of
effective
goodput
degradation,
capturing
both
collision
effects
and
channel
noise,
making
it
the
most
reliable
indicator
for
admission
control
decisions.

The
adaptive
control
loop
adjusts
\(\theta_{BE}\)
every
admission
request:

\textbf{Input:}
Current
utilization
\(A\),
threshold
\(\theta_{BE}\)
\(PER \gets\)
Calculate
from
Eq.
(\protect\hyperlink{eq:per}{{[}eq:per{]}})
\(\theta_{BE} \gets \max(0.80, \theta_{BE} - 0.01)\)
\(\theta_{BE} \gets \min(0.98, \theta_{BE} + 0.01)\)
\textbf{Return:}
Updated
\(\theta_{BE}\)

This
creates
a
closed-loop
feedback
system
that
maximizes
efficiency
while
protecting
QoS.

\hypertarget{analytical-model}{%
\section{Analytical
Model}\label{analytical-model}}

We
develop
a
multi-rate
Erlang
loss
model
to
validate
simulation
results.
Consider
a
system
with
\(C\)
traffic
classes,
each
with
offered
load
\(\rho_c = \lambda_c / \mu_c\)
where
\(\lambda_c\)
is
arrival
rate
and
\(\mu_c\)
is
service
rate.

The
blocking
probability
for
class
\(c\)
is
approximated
by:

\[P_B^c = \frac{\sum_{n \in \mathcal{B}_c} p(n)}{\sum_{n \in \mathcal{S}} p(n)}
\label{eq:erlang_blocking}\]

where
\(\mathcal{B}_c\)
is
the
set
of
states
where
class
\(c\)
would
be
blocked,
\(\mathcal{S}\)
is
the
state
space,
and
\(p(n)\)
is
the
steady-state
probability
of
state
\(n\).

For
our
three-class
system
with
airtime-based
admission,
the
state
space
is
constrained
by
Eq.
(\protect\hyperlink{eq:soft_admission}{{[}eq:soft\_admission{]}}).
We
compute
blocking
probabilities
numerically
and
compare
with
simulation
results
in
Section
V.

